00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.849
Leah: So, what are the
functional and practical

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.940
benefits of using pure functions?

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.600
Let’s have a look at these in more detail.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.600
Jack: Pure functions are easier to
read as they are self-contained,

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:15.500
as all inputs and dependencies
are provided within the function.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.220
To understand the function, you do
not have to look through the code

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.640
for variables which are affected by
the function call, like you might

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.670
have to with an impure function.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.799
Leah: The same function can be reused
in different contexts as it only depends

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:31.672
on what occurs inside the function.

00:00:31.672 --> 00:00:35.527
In contrast, you might have to
write a separate ‘impure function’

00:00:35.527 --> 00:00:40.250
for each global variable that is
changed, rather than relying on pure

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.580
functions which do not access these.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:47.179
Jack: Pure functions have no side
effects, making them much easier to test.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.690
We only need to check the state of
internal variables, not global variables.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:52.370
Referential transparency.

00:00:52.709 --> 00:00:57.500
Leah: When testing a program, the more
processes that run, the longer it takes.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.360
You might want to test a program
but not run certain resource-heavy

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.120
functions which make it take longer.

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:08.299
Referential transparency is when you
can replace a function with whatever the

00:01:08.309 --> 00:01:12.479
output would , without making any other
parts of the program run differently.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.490
This compiles faster
and helps readability.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:20.490
Jack: Caching refers to using techniques
to store results of functions, so

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.480
they only need to be calculated once.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.910
As pure function’s outputs are
always constant, caching is

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.389
possible with pure functions.

